  "title": "ICE Official Says Houston Mayor’s Order Could Shield MS-13 Members From Immigration Arrest",
  "deck": "Mayor John Whitmire’s order ends ICE notifications during routine stops that end in release. Houston’s ICE director says that could cost agents a chance to pick up people with violent records. How often did those handoffs actually happen?",
  "category": "politics",
  "categoryLabel": "Houston",
  "type": "article",
  "author": "EP News",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T16:40:11.390Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-23T16:40:11.390Z",
  "image": {
    "src": "assets/images/houston-ice-ms13-whitmire-20261123.jpg",
    "alt": "File photo of Houston Mayor John Whitmire speaking at a Veterans Day ceremony in 2024.",
    "credit": "File photo: Ed Uthman / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0, cropped."
  },
  "body": [
    "An MS-13 member with a prior homicide conviction and a final order of removal could be stopped for a traffic violation in Houston, get a ticket and drive away without local police notifying federal immigration agents. That’s the scenario Houston ICE Field Office Director Brett Bradford laid out after Mayor John Whitmire changed how the Houston Police Department works with ICE. Bradford offered it as a hypothetical. The city’s new order shows why it could happen.",
    "Under Whitmire’s Nov. 6 order, HPD contacts ICE when a person is already being booked into jail on a new criminal charge or an outstanding criminal warrant and a database check flags an ICE administrative warrant. If the encounter ends with a citation or another release from police custody, the order bars HPD from notifying ICE or extending the stop solely for an immigration pickup. A prior conviction alone does not meet the booking requirement.",
    "Under the previous policy, Bradford told Breitbart Texas, Houston officers could hold someone for up to 30 minutes to give ICE a chance to arrive. He acknowledged that agents often had little time to get across town. His argument is that even a short window gave ICE a chance to take someone into custody while Houston police already had the person stopped. Federal agents can still make their own arrests.",
    "## The MS-13 arrest behind the warning",
    "During the ride-along described in Breitbart’s report, ICE arrested Samuel Jeremias Grande-Caminos, a Salvadoran man the agency identified as an MS-13 member. Bradford said Grande-Caminos had previously been deported and had a federal conviction for illegal reentry. Bradford pointed to him as an example of the kind of person ICE could miss if HPD encounters him during a stop that ends in release. The report documents his arrest by ICE and gives no example of Houston police releasing him or another identified MS-13 member under the new order.",
    "## What Houston gave up",
    "Whitmire said the change was meant to reduce fear and anxiety in Houston. His order says the city wants officers focused on local public safety and wants residents to trust and cooperate with police. It keeps ICE notifications for people who meet the booking and warrant criteria. Bradford’s concern is about the cases that fall outside those criteria, including someone with a serious criminal history who has no current criminal charge or warrant requiring a trip to jail.",
    "The question Houston can answer with records is how often the old policy made a difference. **How many times did ICE actually arrive within 30 minutes, and how many of those handoffs involved documented gang members or people with violent convictions?** The order confirms that Houston closed the roadside handoff route. Those numbers would show how much ICE relied on it."
  ],
